  <h1>Slip &amp; Fall Misconceptions</h1>
  <h2>"If I fell on their property, they automatically owe me money."</h2>
  <p>False. You must prove negligence — that the owner knew or should have known about the hazard and failed to fix it. Falls from open and obvious hazards (a clearly visible step, bright sunshine causing glare) often don't succeed.</p>
  <h2>"Wet floor signs eliminate all liability."</h2>
  <p>Not entirely. A wet floor sign reduces but doesn't eliminate liability. If the sign was inadequate or improperly placed, or if the hazard was more dangerous than a sign could warn about, liability may still attach.</p>
  <h2>"I can't recover if I was partly at fault."</h2>
  <p>In most states, comparative fault rules allow partial recovery. Only a few states still use contributory negligence, which bars recovery if you were any percent at fault.</p>
  <h2>"I have plenty of time to file."</h2>
  <p>Statutes of limitations are strict. Missing the deadline (often 2–3 years, sometimes less for government defendants) permanently bars your claim. Don't delay.</p>
  <h2>"Slip and fall cases are all frivolous."</h2>
  <p>The stereotype is misleading. Serious injuries from preventable falls cause significant medical costs and lost income. Many cases involve real negligence and genuine harm.</p>
</BaseLayout>
